<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Ma'mun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 813–833</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Mamun was an Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> who reigned from 813 to 833. He came to power after a civil war against his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a>. He is famous for his patronage of science and learning, including the translation of Greek works into Arabic, which made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> a leading intellectual centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-mamun.jpg" alt="al-Ma'mun" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Abbasid_Caliph_al-Ma%27mun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This dual legitimacy was central to Abbasid ideology, and <b>al-Ma'mun</b> (r. 813–833) pushed it furthest by adopting Mu'tazilism as state doctrine and launching the mihna, an inquisition asserting the caliph's right to define theological orthodoxy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aghlabids adopted the Mu'tazilite rationalist doctrine after the caliph <b>al-Ma'mun</b> (813–833) made it official in Baghdad, and imposed it as the state doctrine of Ifriqiya, though the Maliki majority rejected its teaching that the Qur'an was created rather than God's eternal word.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Within two generations, under Harun al-Rashid (r. 786–809) and <b>al-Ma'mun</b> (r. 813–833), the caliphate presided over the richest and most sophisticated society in western Eurasia.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>Abu Dulaf al-Qasim ibn Isa, of the Arab Banu Ijl, was a general and poet in the service of Harun al-Rashid and <b>al-Ma'mun</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dulafid-dynasty">Dulafid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Mazyar ibn Qarin converted to Islam and served <b>al-Ma'mun</b>, but rebelled against the caliph al-Mu'tasim and the Tahirid dynasty in 839.</blockquote><a href="/state/qarinvand-dynasty">Qarinvand dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 819 the Abbasid caliph <b>al-Ma'mun</b> gave his four grandsons governorships of Samarkand, Ferghana, Chach and Herat.</blockquote><a href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a></li></ul></section>
